function isPrime(number) {
  // Check special cases (1 or 2)
  if (number === 1 || number === 2) return true;

  for (let i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(number); i++) {
    if (number % i === 0) return false;
  }

  return true;
}

function main(numbers) {
  for (let i = 0; i < numbers.length; i++) {
    let number = numbers[i];

    if (isPrime(number)) {
      console.log(`Number ${number} is prime.`);
      break;
    }
  }
}

// Example usage:
const numbers = [3, 4, 7, 11, 18];

main(numbers);
```

In this code, `isPrime` is a helper function that checks whether a given number is prime. It first handles special cases where the number is either 1 or 2. Then, it iterates from 2 to the square root of the given number and checks if the number is divisible by any of these iterated values.

The `main` function takes an array of numbers and loops through each number checking if it is prime using the `isPrime` function. If it finds a prime number, it prints the result to the console and breaks out of the loop.

